{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B009",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B021",
    "B029"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration from B021 to B009. Optical lattice clocks satisfy the demanding test of a defined physical principle made robust in experiment: the magic-wavelength approach controls the central trapping perturbation while retaining the statistical advantage of neutral-atom ensembles. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's defining experimental development form a coherent two-person account, and the platform's performance has been independently reproduced over many years. This recognizes the established clock architecture and precision-measurement regime, rather than prospective redefinition of the second or downstream applications.",
    "case_against": "The supplied discussion does not itself provide the primary historical record needed finally to establish that Katori and Ye are the only indispensable originators. The citation must therefore remain tightly limited to optical lattice clocks, not the whole optical-frequency-metrology enterprise or its later community-wide improvements. B021 remains scientifically profound, but its proposed citation joins general quantum error correction with topological fault tolerance and confronts both incomplete useful-scale fault-tolerant realization and a broader set of plausible foundational claimants.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that Katori's magic-wavelength concept, Ye's field-defining realizations, and independent reproducibility make B009 exceptionally mature.",
        "response": "I agree, and this has changed my assessment. From the standpoint of an experimental-theory interface, repeated quantitative realization of the defining physical promise carries substantial weight. The appropriate restraint is Kroll's own one: recognize the lattice-clock architecture, not every later metrological advance."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that B009 is a mature advance in precision measurement while warning that the original architecture must be separated from the wider optical-clock community's later work.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. The achievement is stronger when expressed as a new regime for measuring atomic energy differences, rather than through still-developing applications. Her attribution warning is also decisive: archival review must test whether another early contributor has an equal indispensable claim before a final award is made."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig holds that Shor, Steane, and Kitaev are individually foundational, but that a three-person B021 allocation necessarily omits other major contributors.",
        "response": "I agree. My prior configuration was defensible only with a deliberately narrow citation, but the remaining conceptual boundary between general codes and Kitaev's topological route, together with the incomplete experimental maturity of useful-scale fault tolerance, now makes B009 the more bounded final public configuration. This does not diminish the basic importance of quantum error correction."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether primary historical evidence confirms that Katori and Ye alone encompass the indispensable conceptual and experimental origins of the optical-lattice-clock architecture.",
      "How narrowly the B009 citation can be drawn so that it excludes the broader optical-metrology enterprise and later technical improvements.",
      "Whether B021 could be recast around a more unified and fairly attributable foundational achievement."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "As director of WACQT and a Chalmers researcher in superconducting quantum circuits, I have a professional connection to the wider quantum-computing field relevant to alternative B021; no direct connection to its proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. No comparable connection to the proposed B009 laureates is apparent."
  }
}
